# PHP create PDF invoice

Generating a PDF invoice in PHP often starts with a simple requirement: a header, an itemized list of products, and a clean table structure. While basic PDF generation is straightforward, achieving a "nicely formatted" result that mimics professional accounting software requires moving beyond raw binary data manipulation into the realm of layout management. The core challenge isn't just creating the file; it's managing the complexity of page breaks, font rendering, and precise positioning without reinventing the wheel with low-level libraries like `FPDF` or `TCPDF`.

The most pragmatic approach for modern PHP applications is leveraging a high-level library that abstracts these complexities. The `Dompdf` library remains a stalwart choice because it uses HTML and CSS to define the layout. This allows developers to write standard HTML tables and style them with CSS, then render the result into a PDF. You define the structure using familiar tags, and the engine handles the conversion. Alternatively, `mPDF` or `Snappy` (which wraps wkhtmltopdf) are viable options if your project demands specific browser-based rendering capabilities or complex CSS support that Dompdf cannot natively handle.

To implement this with `Dompdf`, you first need to load the library via Composer and instantiate the renderer. The workflow involves loading an HTML template string, configuring the PDF settings such as paper size and margins, and finally invoking the render method. Here is a conceptual example of how you might structure the PHP logic:

```php
require 'vendor/autoload.php';

use Dompdf\Dompdf;

// Instantiate the renderer
$dompdf = new Dompdf();

// Define your invoice content using standard HTML/CSS
$html = '<html>
    <head>
        <style>
            table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
            th, td { border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 8px; text-align: left; }
            th { background-color: #f2f2f2; }
        </style>
    </head>
    <body>
        <h1>Invoice #001</h1>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Qty</th><th>Price</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Widget A</td><td>10</td><td>$50.00</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </body>
</html>';

// Load HTML into the DOM
$dompdf->loadHtml($html);

// Set paper size and margins
$dompdf->setPaper('A4', 'portrait');

// Render to PDF
$dompdf->render();

// Save the result
$dompdf->save("invoice.pdf");
```

This approach decouples your layout logic from the rendering engine. You can maintain a separate HTML file or template string that contains all the styling, ensuring consistency across different instances of the invoice. However, this method has limitations. CSS features supported by browsers are not always supported by the underlying PDF engine; for instance, complex flexbox layouts or advanced animations will fail to render correctly. If you rely on specific font families, ensure they are embedded or available in the system path, as `Dompdf` does not automatically embed fonts from the system without configuration.

A critical trade-off to consider is memory usage and performance. Rendering large documents with many page breaks can be resource-intensive. If your invoice contains thousands of line items, you might encounter memory exhaustion errors during the render phase. In such cases, splitting the generation process or optimizing the HTML payload becomes necessary. Additionally, if you are using Laravel, remember that `Dompdf` is not installed by default; you must manually install it via Composer and ensure the autoloader picks up the classes correctly.

For production environments where strict compliance with PDF standards or specific branding requirements is essential, evaluating commercial libraries or services might be warranted. If your application requires a robust, out-of-the-box solution that handles these edge cases without deep customization, exploring resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) can provide insights into managed solutions or enterprise-grade alternatives tailored for PHP ecosystems. However, for most standard invoicing needs, sticking to open-source renderers with a clear understanding of their CSS limitations remains the most cost-effective strategy.
